<setup>

</setup>
<p>
  Consider the infinite sum involving <m>x</m> given by
  <me>
    T(x) = 1 - 2x + 4x^2 - 8x^3 +  \cdots + (-1)^{n-1} 2^n x^n + \cdots
  </me>
  We explore some different values of <m>x</m> to see why this expression only makes sense for a certain set of numbers <m>x</m>.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Let <m>x = \frac{1}{5}</m> and consider <m>T\left(\frac{1}{5}\right)</m>, which corresponds to the infinite sum
        <me>
          T\left(\frac{1}{5}\right) = 1 - \frac{2}{5} + \frac{4}{25} - \frac{8}{125} +  \cdots
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Explain why this series is geometric and determine whether or not the series converges by finding <m>a</m> and <m>r</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>a = </m></label>1</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>r = </m></label>-2/5</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <choiceInput inline name="S1"><label>This geometric series </label>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>converges</choice>
          <choice>does not converge</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        and its sum is <mathInput name="S1Sum"><label><m>T\left(\frac{1}{5}\right) = </m></label></mathInput>. (Enter <q><em>DNC</em></q> if the series does not converge).
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $S1.selectedIndices=2 and $S1Sum=5/7
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Recall that
        <me>
          T(x) = 1 - 2x + 4x^2 - 8x^3 +  \cdots + (-1)^{n-1} 2^n x^n + \cdots
        </me>
        Now let <m>x = \frac{3}{5}</m> and consider <m>T\left(\frac{3}{5}\right)</m>, which corresponds to the infinite sum
        <me>
          T\left(\frac{3}{5}\right) = 1 - \frac{6}{5} + \frac{36}{25} - \frac{216}{125} +  \cdots
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Explain why this series is geometric and determine whether or not the series converges by finding <m>a</m> and <m>r</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>a = </m></label>1</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>r = </m></label>-6/5</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <choiceInput inline name="S2"><label>This geometric series </label>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>converges</choice>
          <choice>does not converge</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        and its sum is <mathInput name="S2Sum"><label><m>T\left(\frac{3}{5}\right) = </m></label></mathInput>. (Enter <q><em>DNC</em></q> if the series does not converge).
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><award><when>
          $S2.selectedIndices=3 and $S2Sum=DNC
        </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Our work in (a) and (b) shows that for some <m>x</m>-values, <m>T(x)</m> is a geometric series that converges, while for other <m>x</m>-values,
        <me>
          T(x) = 1 - 2x + 4x^2 - 8x^3 +  \cdots + (-1)^{n-1} 2^n x^n + \cdots
        </me>
        is a geometric series that does not converge.  We now want to characterize all <m>x</m>-values for which <m>T(x)</m> converges.  First, identify <m>a</m> and <m>r</m> for <m>T(x)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>a = </m></label>1</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>r = </m></label>-2x</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Now, recall that an infinite geometric series converges if and only if <m>|r| \lt 1</m>.  For what values of <m>x</m> is it true that <m>|r| \lt 1</m> for the geometric series <m>T(x)</m>? Enter your result using interval notation:  if you find the series converges for <m>1 \lt x \le 3</m>, enter <q><m>(1,3]</m></q>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>T(x)</m> converges for all <m>x</m> in the interval </label><interval>(-1/2,1/2)</interval></answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, use the fact that the sum of a convergent geometric series is <m>S = \frac{a}{1-r}</m> to find a single fraction that is equal to <m>T(x)</m> for all <m>x</m> in the interval for which <m>T(x)</m> converges.
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>T(x) = </m></label>1/(1+2x)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>